From ac592d2f99d1f425cebb1093e4b0786bd59c96a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:26:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: what another DRM master left on the output is
 put back with the first commit

Kodi holds DRM master for the whole life of its process, so a program that has the display
before it (a Wayland session that the Flatpak add-on starts after stopping Kodi) is another
process, and what it leaves is what Kodi finds when it starts. The kernel keeps the state of
the connector and the CRTC across programs. Kodi set "max bpc" only where it manages the
depth of the link (nvidia-drm with NV_OUTPUT_BPC, HDMI of i915 and xe), and only across the
8-bit line, so on amdgpu, on DisplayPort of i915 and xe, and on i915 HDMI with 10 left where
12 goes, a depth that the other program had lowered stayed: HDR10 went out as RGB of 8 bits
(CroqueMr #103, LibreELEC forum 30675).

On the first commit of Kodi on an output, "max bpc" is put back to the top of its range
(which is what the kernel gives a connector, drm_connector_attach_max_bpc_property()) on
amdgpu, i915 and xe, wherever the link is not held at 8 on purpose. The CRTC properties that
Kodi never sets and that a compositor may leave (CTM, GAMMA_LUT, DEGAMMA_LUT, VRR_ENABLED)
are cleared when they are set. A clean output goes out as before: nothing is added. The log
says what was found. The HDR metadata, the colorimetry and the RGB range were not assumed:
SetHDR(nullptr) and SetColorimetry(nullptr) queue theirs at start, and CRgbRange reads the
kernel's value.

Host test: foreign_test.cpp (30 checks, and a mutation of the
header that it catches); the earlier test of LinkDepth.h (18733 checks) still passes.
---
